00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
nato secretary general jens stoltenberg  welcoming the ambassadors of the two  countries that want to become the newest  members to join an alliance which  currently has 30 members  the ambassadors had with them their  formal letters of application  this moment marking a radical change in  opinion in the two nordic nations that  has long cherished their non-aligned  status  a change that's taken place in a period  of less than three months since russia  invaded ukraine  every nation has the right to choose its  own path  you have both made your choice of the  thorough democratic processes  and i warmly welcome  the requests by finland and sweden to  join nato  you are  our closest partners  and your membership  in nato would increase our shared  security of the current nato members 29  out of 30 strongly support sweden and  finland's membership they both have  sophisticated armies substantial fleets  of aircraft and both have served  alongside nato forces in the balkans and  afghanistan  the leader of one nato member though has  objections turkey's president erdogan  accuses both countries of harboring  terrorists in particular kurdish  nationalist groups the us is confident  that turkey can be persuaded and that's  why the u.s secretary of state will be  meeting his turkish counterparts soon at  the u.n in new york if turkey does  change its position the two countries  could be sitting around the nato table  as invitee nations at a summit at the  end of june  formal ratification though will take  several months more  in a significant move the u.s aircraft  carrier the uss harry truman and its  strike group have been moved in recent  hours to the baltic sea  finland and sweden are not yet members  covered by nato's security guarantees  but its presence is a clear sign to  russia if it was considering any  response to the latest developments  james bay's al jazeera at nato  headquarters in brussels
 

